19
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:15,440
Does anyone know what we celebrate on May 1st?

20
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:16,440
Cinco de Mayo.

21
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,480
No, that's May 5th in Spanish, James.

22
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:28,440
No wonder you're failing my Spanish class.

23
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:31,380
No, May 1st is International Workers' Day.

24
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:33,140
Do we get a day off from school then?

25
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:34,120
No.

26
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:39,660
It is not considered to be a national holiday here in the US, but in other countries it is.

27
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:41,520
Oh man.

28
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:48,740
In the 19th century, working conditions were appalling with workers being forced to work 10, 12, and 14 hours a day.

29
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:56,880
Support for the 8-hour workday movement was growing rapidly, despite the indifference and hostility of many union leaders.

30
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:03,400
And by April 1886, 250,000 workers were involved in the May Day movement.

31
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:09,370
Previous legislative attempts to improve working conditions had failed, so labor organizers took drastic measures.

32
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:14,620
They passed a resolution stating that 8 hours would constitute a legal day's work.

33
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:17,890
And on May 1st, 1886, the resolution took effect.

34
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:19,720
Cool.

35
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:21,170
Is that why we only work 8 hours now?

36
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:23,120
Yes.

37
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:24,580
But the happy ending came at a high price.

38
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:33,100
On May 3rd, 1886, police fired into a crowd of strikers at the McCormick Reaper Works factory, killing four and wounding many.

39
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:36,840
A mass meeting was called for the next day to protest the brutality.

40
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:38,580
And then what happened?

41
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:41,440
Well, as we say, the rest is history.

42
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:49,520
All right.

