00:07:36,950 --> 00:07:37,450
Okay.

188
00:07:37,490 --> 00:07:43,230
So we have some phrases that we saw in this dialogue and well, let's start with the first one.

189
00:07:43,470 --> 00:07:51,550
Well, the teacher opens his discussion by talking about how an understanding of the legal system is an important aspect of being a good citizen.

190
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Okay.

191
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:54,140
An important aspect.

192
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:54,790
An important aspect.

193
00:07:55,140 --> 00:07:57,160
So, well, maybe we know the word important, right?

194
00:07:57,160 --> 00:07:57,810
So what's an aspect?

195
00:07:58,160 --> 00:08:00,920
Obviously, a part of something, an important part of something.

196
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:01,070
Okay.

197
00:08:01,570 --> 00:08:06,890
So the teacher started with an important aspect of and then specified the idea or what part it belongs to.

198
00:08:07,050 --> 00:08:07,170
Right.

199
00:08:07,390 --> 00:08:08,850
So it usually follows this form.

200
00:08:09,290 --> 00:08:12,230
Noun is an important aspect of noun.

201
00:08:12,750 --> 00:08:13,050
Okay.

202
00:08:13,390 --> 00:08:19,130
So for example, quality control is an important aspect of keeping customers happy.

203
00:08:19,450 --> 00:08:19,750
Okay.

204
00:08:19,890 --> 00:08:20,190
Perfect.

205
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:22,520
A part of.

206
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:23,520
Very good.

207
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:26,240
So it's a lot more formal or just a lot more intelligent, right?

208
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:26,530
Yeah, professional.

209
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:30,250
And actually, this is a wonderful way of setting up your idea.

210
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:36,430
So the teacher said that it's an important aspect of understanding the legal system was understanding its origins or its genesis.

211
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:42,880
And then she moved on and said that it's not enough to simply memorize laws.

212
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:43,000
That's right.

213
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:45,700
It's not enough to simply do something.

214
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:47,240
Okay.

215
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:50,680
So the beginning of this sentence, it is not enough to simply something.

216
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:55,400
By starting a sentence like this, you are indicating that more is required, right?

217
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:58,620
So here, it's not enough to simply memorize the laws.

218
00:08:59,440 --> 00:09:03,580
We're saying that most people just memorize the laws, but they need to go further.

219
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:04,740
And understand them.

220
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:06,240
Exactly.

221
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:11,520
Another example would be, it's not enough to simply pass your exams.

222
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:13,700
You must get good grades as well.

223
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:14,560
Right.

224
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:15,900
Or maybe in a business context.

225
00:09:16,740 --> 00:09:19,040
It's not enough to simply satisfy your customers.

226
00:09:19,580 --> 00:09:20,660
You must impress them.

227
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:21,280
Okay.

228
00:09:21,860 --> 00:09:22,140
Very good.

229
00:09:22,460 --> 00:09:26,480
So that's a very good way of starting out this idea of more is required.

230
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:27,140
That's right.

231
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:27,860
More is required.

232
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:29,260
All right.

233
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:31,040
All right.

234
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:32,280
Well, I want to move on to one final point here.

235
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:34,820
I found this sentence to be really, really interesting.

236
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:41,560
They passed a resolution stating that eight hours would constitute a legal day's work.

237
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:42,480
Okay.

238
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:44,480
So why is this interesting?

239
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:48,460
Well, I found that, you know, there's two verbs going on here.

240
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:55,480
They passed a resolution that eight hours would constitute a legal day's work.

241
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:58,150
So we've got two verb times going on here at the same time.

242
00:09:58,150 --> 00:10:00,430
And I'm wondering about this one would.

243
00:10:00,930 --> 00:10:01,410
Okay.

244
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:03,440
So what's happening here?

245
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:04,630
Obviously this happened in the past.

246
00:10:04,720 --> 00:10:06,160
It's like about a hundred years ago, right?

247
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,160
Right.

248
00:10:07,160 --> 00:10:10,080
So the resolution was passed a hundred years ago.

249
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:10,320
Right.

250
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:12,300
So this is going on in the past.

251
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:13,480
Right.

252
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:17,320
But there was a future action that was going to happen in consequence of this.

253
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:18,320
Right.

254
00:10:18,320 --> 00:10:22,000
So the resolution stated that eight hours would constitute.

255
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:27,220
So that's like the primary action was the passing of the resolution.

256
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:34,680
And the result is this sort of this event that happens in the past, but is a future event to the first primary event.

257
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:36,160
Does that make any sense at all?

258
00:10:36,610 --> 00:10:38,390
So this is called the future in the past, right?

259
00:10:38,490 --> 00:10:38,690
That's right.

260
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:39,440
Okay.

261
00:10:39,610 --> 00:10:40,990
So it's a grammar structure.

262
00:10:41,470 --> 00:10:47,530
Many of you maybe know the simple present or the future tenses, but this is a little strange one.

263
00:10:47,810 --> 00:10:51,470
As we said, so maybe it's a little bit difficult to understand the grammar in general.

264
00:10:51,470 --> 00:10:56,110
So why don't we take a look at some more examples of how we would use this future in the past.

